  "query_preview": "..Today through Monday...Quiet weather is forecast\nthrough early next week. Expect dry conditions and daytime \ntemperatures generally 5-15 degrees above normal each day through \nMonday. A pair of dry shortwaves will brush past northern Arizona \nthrough the period, with the only impacts being cloudiness and \noccasional breezy conditions. Localized gusts up to 25-35 mph will\nbe possible along/north of the Mogollon Rim and White Mountains \ntoday and Monday.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday...A strong Pacific low-pressure system is \nforecast impact northern Arizona, bringing the potential for \nsignificant rainfall, high elevation snow, and gusty winds. Model \nguidance continues to fall into consensus on the broad features of \nthis system. The disturbance is forecast to strengthen and drift \nsoutheastward across the East Pacific in the preceding days. \nBefore the moisture arrives, gradients aloft rapidly strengthen \non Tuesday bringing daytime wind gusts as high as 40-50 mph. \nPrecipitation chances ramp up late Tuesday as an initial surge of\nsubtropical moisture arrives. The heaviest precipitation amounts \nare expected overnight Tuesday into Wednesday, with lingering \nrain/high elevation snow showers possible into Wednesday night. \n\nThe distribution of rainfall accumulations will be heavily \ninfluenced by orography, with much heavier amounts along/south of \nthe Mogollon Rim. The NBM mean precipitation accumulations continue \nto trend higher, currently sitting around 1.0-2.5'' along/south of \nthe Rim and 0.3-0.6'' north of the Rim. Soils are still fairly \nsaturated from hydrological events a few weeks ago, with CPC \nestimated soil moisture currently sitting above the 90-95th \npercentile. This will act to increase runoff from any rain that does \nfall, and there is the potential for flooding of area streams/rivers \nwith this event. The WPC has introduced a Slight risk of excessive \nrainfall for Yavapai, northern Gila, and southern Coconino county on \nTuesday due to the evening/night precipitation chances, and the \nSlight moves eastward on Wednesday. Snow levels have trended a \nlittle higher with this storm, generally staying at or above 7000 \nfeet through the duration. Nevertheless, this could bring travel \nimpacts to the higher terrain.\n\nThere is room for most of these numbers to change as the fine \ndetails of this storm resolve over the coming days. Ensemble \nclusters still maintain realistic lower-end and higher-end \nscenarios, so be sure to check back for future updates!\n\nThursday and onward...Conditions are expected to dry out on Thursday \nas the low fully moves eastward out of our area. Long term guidance \nhints at the pattern remaining active into the weekend and the \npotential for a few more rounds of rain/snow, so stay tuned.",
